  "semantic_checks": [
    "The actual total-degree finite index set is the Finsupp antidiagonal for Fin(d+1), with membership w_0 + sum tail = n and cardinality choose(n+d,d). The grouped polynomial is the literal finite sum of embedded terms.",
    "Its degree-n homogeneity and coefficient L1 bound M choose(n+d,d) T^n follow from actual single-term homogeneity/nonexpansivity and the exact finite count.",
    "The binomial coefficient factor does not cause an exponential radius loss: summability of choose(n+d,d)(DR)^n is proved for every DR<1 and used to bound the actual formal-series radius. For a point with D||x||<1, a strictly intermediate R is chosen; D=0 has its own positive-radius construction.",
    "The grouped analytic series uses actual multilinear representatives whose diagonal evaluations equal the actual grouped polynomial evaluations and whose norms are bounded by actual coefficient L1.",
    "The original isotropic sum is the literal ungrouped product-index tsum. Absolute summability at each point of T||z||<1 is derived using the closed-polydisc majorant. The proved reindexing theorem then identifies it with the grouped analytic sum. No equality or convergence premise is substituted for this derivation.",
    "AnalyticOnNhd.congr is applied on the actual open isotropic domain, so equality on that domain suffices for the local analytic conclusion.",
    "The rescaled polynomial family multiplies A_m,gamma by the actual scalar a^m b^(sum gamma). Exact homogeneous evaluation proves its termwise identity with the original family at scaled coordinates.",
    "The coefficient bound uses exact scalar L1 homogeneity and D a <= T, S b <= T, with nonnegativity of T derived explicitly. It retains both rates until the common local rate T is chosen.",
    "The block map scales actual Sum.inl and Sum.inr coordinates independently. It is complex analytic coordinate by coordinate, has the proved inverse cancellation for nonzero a,b, and its inverse maps the two coordinate bounds to full sup norm <=1.",
    "For each point of the separate domain D||X||<1 and S||s||<1, the proof chooses positive a>||X|| and b>||s|| with D a<1 and S b<1. Then T=max(D a,S b)<1 and the inverse-scaled point lies in the isotropic analytic domain.",
    "Composition with the actual inverse block map is justified at that exact inverse-scaled point by comp_of_eq. The resulting function is globally termwise equal to the original tsum, so analytic transport has the correct target function.",
    "D=0, S=0, zero coefficient families, constant terms and d=0 are included. No inverse of D or S is used in the coordinate map; only the chosen positive a,b are inverted. The empty spectator block has norm zero.",
    "Expanded hypotheses contain actual homogeneity and actual coefficient L1 budgets only. They do not assume original-series convergence, analytic descent, a physical Taylor family, a coordinate branch or the target conclusion."
